 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dey0GCHogK-/",
 "title": "Give Feedback Without Upsetting Someone Using Stories",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator shares a story about how to give feedback to a valuable star employee without causing defensiveness. The main lesson is that packaging feedback inside a relevant story makes it less personal and easier to accept.",
 "key_points": [
  "A star employee brought in 60-70 percent of the order book and felt rules did not apply to him, often arriving late to meetings.",
  "Instead of a hard confrontation, the manager shared the story of cricketers Sachin Tendulkar and Vinod Kambli.",
  "Kambli was said to be better than Sachin in every format initially, but Sachin always arrived at the practice ground 30 to 45 minutes early while Kambli was late.",
  "The manager asked the employee: \"Do you want to be Sachin or do you want to be Vinod Kambli?\"",
  "The creator claims that feedback given in a story form is accepted far more voluntarily and with less defensiveness than directly pointing out a flaw."
 ],
 "content": "Give feedback without upsetting someone\n\nThis is a story told to me by a friend of mine many, many years ago. He had a star employee and the star employee was responsible for almost 60-70% of the order book and therefore had a little bit of a you know rules don't apply to me kind of a thing. So his timesheet wouldn't arrive on time. He'd often be late for meetings and all of that. But he kind of went with the flow because he knew that he was a star employee. Now, my friend had an option of giving him a hard talking to or telling him a story. And this is what he did. He told him the story of two cricketers who sort of entered the Indian cricket scene more or less at the same time, Sachin Tendulkar and Vinod Kambli. I don't know much about cricket, but they say that Kambli was possibly better than Sachin in every format of the game at that point of time. Better in 20/20, one day, test, whatever. The only difference was Sachin would always come to the practice ground about half an hour, 45 minutes early, which he still does by the way. Whereas Kambli would come a little late. My friend just looked at his employee and told him, at a star employee and told him, the rest is up to you. Do you want to be Sachin or do you want to be Vinod Kambli? Now, in many ways, I think the narration of a story or feedback that is packed in a story makes it not very personal to that individual. And that is why feedback given in a story form usually is accepted far more voluntarily and far less defensively than pointing out a flaw in that particular person. Stories are a great way to give feedback.\n\nOn screen text:\nGive feedback without upsetting someone",
 "status": "done",
